6. DAILY TXT FILE SYSTEM

Create one TXT file per category per local calendar day.

Examples:

"House for Rent/2026-11-26.txt"

"Land for Sale/2026-11-26.txt"

If the user submits multiple properties in the same category on the same day, append all records to the existing daily file.

Never create a separate TXT file for every submission.

When the local calendar date changes, create a new daily file automatically.

Use the device's local date and time. Do not use UTC date conversion.

TXT RECORD FORMAT

Use a consistent, reliable format similar to:

========================================
PROPERTY RECORD
RECORD_ID: HOUSE_000001
DATE: 2026-11-26
TIME: 09:15 AM
CATEGORY: HOUSE_FOR_RENT
MOBILE: 017XXXXXXXX
DETAILS:
Iswarganj, Mymensingh.
Two bedroom house available.
Monthly rent negotiable.

For land records:

========================================
PROPERTY RECORD
RECORD_ID: LAND_000001
DATE: 2026-11-26
TIME: 10:20 AM
CATEGORY: LAND_FOR_SALE
MOBILE: 017XXXXXXXX
DETAILS:
Land available near main road.
Price negotiable.

These are format examples only. Actual DETAILS must contain the exact text supplied by the user.

Record ID requirements

- Each record must have a unique and stable ID.
- House and land records must have separate ID prefixes.
- IDs must not reset when a new day starts.
- IDs must never be reused after deletion or failure.
- Store the record ID in the TXT file.
- Use the same ID to identify its generated poster.
- Use the ID to prevent duplicate poster generation.

For example:

"HOUSE_000001"

"HOUSE_000002"

"LAND_000001"

This prevents filename collisions across different dates.

Data safety

- Never overwrite an existing daily TXT file.
- Never delete previous records automatically.
- Never silently discard an incomplete or failed write.
- Use safe append operations.
- Handle unexpected app termination carefully.
- Show clear error messages if storage is unavailable.
- If TXT saving fails, do not report a successful submission.

